{"schema": "gce.chatgpt.normalized.v1", "conversation_id": "6ab4a1a7-ca60-83e8-a7db-c38d88fc2191", "title": "NCAA Handoff Summary", "canonical_url": "https://chatgpt.com/g/g-p-6ab18502d1248191852c67e6b66490c0/c/6ab4a1a7-ca60-83e8-a7db-c38d88fc2191", "project_id": "g-p-6ab18502d1248191852c67e6b66490c0", "create_time": 1790222765.107187, "update_time": 1790375539.85224, "default_model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "is_archived": false, "is_temporary_chat": false, "context_truncation_continuation": null, "turn_count": 184, "role_counts": {"user": 5, "assistant": 115, "tool": 64}, "turns": [{"message_id": "7e10b859-2a28-4cb6-b887-ba5d982fda08", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222766.093961, "updated_at": 1790222783.248656, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "EC⁸ EDGE CREW QUANT — NCAA FOOTBALL DETAILED PROJECT HANDOFF\n\nDATE: 2026-09-23\nSESSION: NCAA Football Methodology / Data / Don Delta Handoff\nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance\nCURRENT GATE: Methodology Discovery → Structural Data Research → Next Live Stress Test\nPURPOSE: Move the NCAA Football sandbox into a dedicated project without losing durable methodology state.\nSTATUS: PROCEED — discovery state is mature enough for a dedicated NCAA project; weighting is not yet locked.\n\n1. Handoff Objective\n\nPRIMARY OBJECTIVE: Stand up NCAA Football as a first-class Edge Crew Quant sport branch as MLB moves toward the postseason and regular-season volume declines.\n\nWHAT THIS HANDOFF PRESERVES: The full NCAA methodology discovery state, the first live stress-test findings, the Data Hound package lessons, the Don Delta process design, the source/data gaps, the key postmortems, and the next gates.\n\nWHAT THIS HANDOFF DOES NOT DO: It does not lock NCAA component weights, fair-line transforms, opponent-adjustment formulas, leverage values, or production architecture. Those remain discovery-stage until source sufficiency is resolved.\n\n2. Locked Cross-Sport Lifecycle\n\nBASELINE → STATUS SUMMARY → USER DASHBOARD → DRILL-DOWN → HUMAN INPUT → CLASSIFY / VALIDATE → DON DELTA → MODEL SYNTHESIS → REANALYZE\n\n• Baseline math remains separate from Don Delta.\n• Human input never silently rewrites canonical baseline calculations.\n• Confidence and uncertainty remain visible.\n• Market data is isolated from baseline until market-comparison stage.\n• Winner quality, spread value, total value, loss quality, and leverage-event interpretation remain distinct outputs.\n• Pass / no-play is a legitimate result.\n\n3. NCAA Football Baseline — Discovery Components\n\n1. Offense — QB, run game, receivers, OL, scheme/play-calling, efficiency/explosiveness, error profile, adaptability.\n2. Defense — front, pass rush, run defense, LB play, secondary, pressure, coverage, explosiveness allowed, red-zone defense, adaptability.\n3. Special Teams — kicking, punting, return game, hidden field position, special-teams errors.\n4. Roster / Personnel — talent, transfers, experience, returning production, positional continuity, injuries, depth.\n5. Coaching / Scheme — HC/OC/DC quality, continuity, system maturity, schematic identity, adjustment ability.\n6. Matchup — interaction terms: pressure × QB error, OL × front, receiver profile × coverage, coaching familiarity, pace/style conflicts.\n7. Team Form / Current Performance — current-season efficiency and quality, dynamically opponent-adjusted rather than brand-adjusted.\n8. Schedule / Situational — opponent quality, rest, travel, rivalry/objective context, early-season sample issues.\n9. Venue / Environment — home field, altitude, heat, weather, field/surface, travel/time-zone effects.\n10. Game-State / Style Profile — pace, possession structure, comeback/lead behavior, scoring distribution, margin sustainability.\n11. Discipline / Execution — turnovers, penalties, assignment errors, protection busts, red-zone failures.\n12. Program / Coaching / Historical Continuity — coach tenure, system age, staff overlap, returning starters, transfer/roster continuity.\n13. Market State — spread, total, moneyline, opener/current/close; kept outside baseline until comparison.\n\n4. Core NCAA Methodology Findings\n\n• Opponent adjustment must be embedded, not cosmetic. Program reputation can mislead. Current opponent quality must be dynamic, recursive, and current-season aware.\n• The model cannot be purely additive. The highest-value signal often lives in interactions, not in standalone team scores.\n• QB error/variance must be explicit. INT risk, sack response, pressure response, decision volatility, and rushing escape behavior need their own treatment.\n• Coach quality != system maturity. New HC/OC/DC, limited games together, player familiarity, staff continuity, and installation age must be separated.\n• Possession burden matters. Repeated offensive three-and-outs can degrade defensive performance through snap load, field position, and recovery time.\n• High-leverage events/sequences need an explicit layer. Turnovers, explosives, red-zone possessions, fourth downs, special teams, clock/timeout states, end-of-half drives, and late-game possession sequences can dominate outcome/margin.\n• Margin sustainability is a separate analytical question. A favorite can be clearly superior and still be overpriced against a large spread if the game structure does not support repeated separation.\n• Winner projection and spread value are different.\n• Loss quality / misleading final score must be derivable.\n• Exogenous disruption must be recorded.\n\n5. Don Delta — Locked Process Design\n\nLOCKED PRINCIPLE: Don Delta is a stable cross-sport human-interpretive layer. What changes by sport is the candidate-signal set and the leverage unit.\n\nCRITICAL PROCESS CORRECTION: Don Delta must be conversational and two-sided. Reading a finished board is not enough; the back-and-forth is what exposes missing information, familiarity bias, and confirmation bias.\n\nMandatory Don Delta Conversation\n\n1. Initial Don Lean.\n2. Pro-Lean Case.\n3. Counter-Case.\n4. Failure Conditions.\n5. Information-Asymmetry Check.\n6. Reconciled Don Delta.\n7. Confidence / Uncertainty revision.\n\nNCAA Don Delta Candidate Families\n\n• Program-builder asymmetry / inherited-system exposure\n• Successor counter-familiarity\n• Opponent-specific exploitation\n• Identity maturity vs inherited identity\n• Halftime / tactical adjustment\n• Pressure-induced player error\n• Game-management leverage\n• Clock / timeout management\n• Possession architecture\n• Margin sustainability\n• Big-program underdog resistance\n• Program reputation lag / perception-evidence gap\n• Misleading prior result / loss quality\n• Exogenous disruption context\n\n6. LSU–Ole Miss Stress Test\n\nPregame baseline: provisional LSU -4 to -5.\nDon lean: LSU by roughly six with Kiffin-specific leverage thesis.\nFinal: Ole Miss 32, LSU 24.\n\nPostmortem: Don underweighted Trinidad Chambliss, Kewan Lacy, Ole Miss home context, and current offensive continuity. Don knew Kiffin better than the actual LSU/Ole Miss player layer.\n\nPartially right: LSU materially changed after halftime and came back from 24-7 to 24-24.\n\nFailed: Ole Miss showed enough independent identity and counter-response to win the decisive late sequences.\n\nProcess lesson: The pre-Don board must auto-surface both teams’ key players, continuity, QB risk, schedule quality, injuries, and strongest counter-case.\n\n7. First Live Slate Reconciliation\n\n• Wake Forest @ Miami: Wake + large points covered. Margin sustainability / favorite inflation.\n• FSU @ Alabama: FSU +18.5/+20 covered. Favorite superior, dog still right side; FSU lost turnovers 3-0 and there was a roughly 2.5-hour weather delay.\n• Michigan State @ Notre Dame: MSU around +29.5 covered. Big-program underdog resistance / margin sustainability.\n• Kentucky @ Texas A&M: Kentucky +16.5/+17 won outright. Perception/ranking inflation; efficiency and turnover edge.\n• SMU @ Louisville: SMU side missed. Turnovers 3-0 and explosive efficiency outweighed raw yardage.\n• USC @ Rutgers: Model Rutgers +22.5 was right; Don leaned USC. Need stronger counter-case and player/context pre-load.\n• LSU @ Ole Miss: Baseline/Don LSU missed. Personnel/context underweight; halftime adjustment real, final leverage edge wrong.\n• Utah State @ Utah: Pass. Do not force marginal edges.\n\n8. Data Package — What Was Proven\n\n• Broad NCAA information model is viable.\n• Strong coverage: core team performance, QB profiles, roster/transfer continuity, coaching/staff continuity, broad OL continuity, injuries, situational context, venue/weather, historical context, isolated market snapshots.\n• Conflicts were preserved instead of silently resolved.\n• Market was kept separate from baseline.\n• Schema stability still needs work in some flat-file areas.\n\n9. NCAA RED Data Gaps\n\n• Full FBS play-by-play with stable IDs and historical depth\n• Stable drive data\n• Snap / player participation\n• Pressure / pressure-allowed or validated proxy\n• Reliable targets / route participation\n• Deeper historical continuity and entity resolution\n• Sufficient market time-series depth\n\n10. Data Strategy\n\nREQUIRED VARIABLE → REQUIRED FIELD → CURRENT SOURCE → COVERAGE → QUALITY → LATENCY → PROVENANCE → COST / LICENSING → DERIVABILITY → DECISION\n\nAllowed decisions:\n\n• KEEP\n• DERIVE INTERNALLY\n• BUILD / SCRAPE\n• BUY / LICENSE\n• SUPPLEMENT / MULTI-SOURCE\n• DOWNGRADE TO OPTIONAL\n• STRIP FROM METHODOLOGY\n\nTARGET STANDARD: 99%+ reliable availability for data required by the locked methodology.\n\nECONOMIC PRINCIPLE: Across ~16 sports plus player props, optimize analytical quality × reliability × coverage × maintainability ÷ total cost.\n\n11. Preferred Internal Derivations\n\nEPA/play, success rate, explosive rate, early-down efficiency, passing-down efficiency, points/drive, field-position value, scoring opportunities, garbage-time filters, turnover-adjusted performance, win/loss quality, misleading final score, opponent adjustment, margin sustainability, backdoor-cover profile, coach decision profiles, leverage-event values.\n\n12. Skeptical / Potential Strip Variables\n\nTurnover-worthy plays, proprietary OL grades, missed tackles, coverage grades, motion %, RPO %, man/zone %, route-level charting, detailed pressure attribution.\n\n13. Required Pre-Don Context Board\n\nFor each team:\n\n• key QB\n• RB / primary rushing threat\n• primary receivers\n• OL continuity\n• defensive stars / disruption\n• injuries / availability\n• coaching / staff continuity\n• opponent-adjusted form\n• recent schedule quality\n\nThen surface:\n\n• strongest baseline case for A\n• strongest baseline case for B\n• critical unknowns\n• leverage matchup candidates\n• source-confidence flags\n\n14. Required Human-Facing Output Schema\n\n• Fair line / fair total\n• Market line / total\n• Modeled gap\n• Confidence\n• Uncertainty band\n• Primary drivers\n• Opposing offsets\n• Critical unknowns\n• Don Delta candidates\n• Data gaps / source-quality flags\n• Leverage-event candidates\n• Failure conditions\n\n15. Source Research State\n\nStructural Research / Data Hound should investigate NCAA-wide sourcing for:\nPBP, drives, snaps, pressure, targets/routes, continuity, market time series, opponent-quality inputs, loss-quality inputs, margin-sustainability inputs, leverage events, coach/game-management data, system maturity, and factual home/environment inputs.\n\n16. Governance\n\nDon → Coda → Kimi → Chiave when materially required → Kimi Design & Build → implementation → Talon → Luce → Coda → Don.\n\nDo not code before methodology gates are accepted. Do not silently merge evidence into canon.\n\n17. Canon / Non-Canon\n\nLOCKED\n\n• Baseline separate from Don Delta\n• Don Delta conversational and two-sided\n• Winner projection, spread value, loss quality, leverage interpretation distinct\n• Market isolated until comparison\n• Provenance and uncertainty visible\n\nDISCOVERY\n\n• NCAA weights\n• fair-line transforms\n• coaching-familiarity value\n• leverage-event value\n• opponent-adjustment formula\n• system-maturity coefficient\n\n18. Next Gate in New NCAA Project\n\n1. Ingest this handoff as governing discovery state.\n2. Do not redesign the lifecycle.\n3. Review Data Hound structural source-research return.\n4. Reconcile RED gaps into KEEP / BUILD / DERIVE / BUY / STRIP.\n5. Return to weighting/calibration only after data sufficiency is structurally understood.\n6. Use next live NCAA slate as second stress test.\n7. Auto-populate pre-Don context before asking for a lean.\n8. Lock failure conditions prospectively.\n\n19. Project Pivot\n\nMLB is moving toward postseason mode and regular-season volume is declining. NCAA Football now becomes a primary active Edge Crew Quant development surface. Treat this handoff as the current discovery anchor: strong enough to proceed, not mature enough to freeze weights.\n\n20. Closeout\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED\nCONFIDENCE: 99% on process and discovery-state reconstruction; methodology calibration remains open.\nNEXT GATE: NCAA Structural Data Research → methodology retention decisions → second live stress test.\nDON INPUT NEEDED: No for handoff.", "text_chars": 12163, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e3d34995-e42d-47c4-bcd7-d951616faf5b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222768.846926, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f0bcfa9c-920c-4879-91f0-51cc847c73bc", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222774.145217, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8238df69-86ff-5d50-a0ac-e03afe5152cd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222774.237384, "updated_at": 1790222783.249109, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Understood. I’m treating this handoff as the governing NCAA Football discovery anchor for the EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant project as of September 23, 2026.\n\nFrozen operating state:\n\n- Status: **PROCEED**\n- Current gate: **NCAA Structural Data Research → methodology retention decisions → second live stress test**\n- NCAA Football becomes a primary active development surface as MLB shifts toward postseason mode.\n- The cross-sport lifecycle remains unchanged.\n- Baseline, Don Delta, Model Synthesis, and market comparison remain separate.\n- Don Delta must be conversational, two-sided, and completed only after the pre-Don context board is populated.\n- Winner projection, spread value, total value, loss quality, and leverage interpretation remain distinct outputs.\n- Pass/no-play remains valid.\n- No NCAA weights, fair-line transforms, opponent-adjustment formulas, system-maturity coefficients, or leverage values are locked.\n- No production coding begins before the methodology and data-sufficiency gates are accepted.\n- Evidence enters canon only through explicit reconciliation—never by silent promotion.\n\nThe immediate sequence is:\n\n1. Review the Data Hound structural source-research return.\n2. Map each required variable to actual fields and sources.\n3. Resolve the RED gaps through **KEEP / DERIVE / BUILD / BUY / SUPPLEMENT / OPTIONAL / STRIP**.\n4. Determine whether the methodology is supportable at the required reliability level.\n5. Build the next slate’s pre-Don context board.\n6. Prospectively record failure conditions.\n7. Conduct the second live NCAA stress test.\n8. Revisit weighting and calibration only after structural data sufficiency is understood.\n\nThe LSU–Ole Miss result and the first live slate remain evidence and pressure-test material—not frozen NCAA calibration truth. No additional Don input is required to begin the next gate.", "text_chars": 1851, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fd485768-a387-4673-a731-558be12b33de", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222810.5386682, "updated_at": 1790222882.60814, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, what I want to do now is add to this what I've learned from doing the MLB, just more as an overall process and overall, edge to edge. That's not specific for this, different data analytics.", "text_chars": 195, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2d989e98-e2ff-484e-b482-cff56f1a5f1a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222811.563599, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "97b31e4b-f810-52a5-b5f5-82c6c898a613", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222821.903147, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "977c7187-6255-4bf4-a893-96c1af4e0c19", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222838.352746, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "18c6608c-5e0d-581e-a641-8d6e469c0cc7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790222838.388935, "updated_at": 1790222882.608429, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. The MLB work should inform NCAA through a **cross-sport operating layer**, not by importing MLB calculations, variables, or weights.\n\nThe cleanest move is to preserve the existing NCAA handoff unchanged and append this as a proposed cross-sport addendum:\n\n## Addendum A — Cross-Sport Edge-to-Edge Operating Lessons From MLB\n\n**PURPOSE:** Carry forward durable process lessons proven during MLB development without importing MLB-specific analytics into NCAA Football methodology.\n\n**SCOPE:** This governs the complete evidence lifecycle—acquisition, identity, normalization, derivation, delivery, verification, correction, and archival. It does not determine NCAA component weights, formulas, fair lines, or sport-specific variables.\n\n### A1. Edge-to-Edge Analytical Chain\n\nEvery analytical output must remain traceable across the complete chain:\n\n**SOURCE → ACQUISITION → RAW ARTIFACT → NORMALIZATION → IDENTITY RESOLUTION → POINT-IN-TIME SNAPSHOT → DERIVATION → BASELINE → HUMAN REVIEW → DON DELTA → MODEL SYNTHESIS → OUTPUT → RESULT → POSTMORTEM**\n\nA strong final model cannot compensate for a broken upstream chain. Data correctness, identity integrity, temporal integrity, analytical correctness, and presentation correctness are separate gates.\n\n### A2. Identity Is Foundational\n\nEntity identity must be resolved before analytical use.\n\nThis applies to:\n\n- teams\n- players\n- coaches and staff\n- games and events\n- seasons and weeks\n- venues\n- source-specific identifiers\n- market instruments\n- injuries and availability records\n\nNames alone are not sufficient identifiers. Aliases, transfers, abbreviations, duplicate names, roster changes, and source-ID conflicts must be handled explicitly.\n\nAn unresolved identity must be labeled unresolved; it must never be silently matched to the most convenient candidate.\n\n### A3. Temporal Integrity and Anti-Lookahead\n\nEvery material observation must carry an **as-of time** appropriate to the analysis.\n\nPregame state may not be overwritten with:\n\n- confirmed post-kickoff availability\n- live-game statistics\n- closing information unavailable at decision time\n- postgame corrections\n- future roster or coaching knowledge\n- later market movements presented as earlier knowledge\n\nPregame, live, and postgame states must remain independently recoverable.\n\n### A4. Evidence Classification\n\nMaterial inputs must be classified as:\n\n- **DIRECT** — explicitly reported or measured\n- **DERIVED** — calculated from identified inputs\n- **PROXY** — an imperfect substitute for the desired variable\n- **UNAVAILABLE** — required information not reliably obtained\n- **CONFLICTED** — credible sources disagree\n- **STALE** — information exists but is no longer timely enough for the decision\n\nBinding interpretations:\n\n- **PROXY ≠ DIRECT**\n- **MISSING ≠ ZERO**\n- **STALE ≠ CURRENT**\n- **DISCOVERABLE ≠ VERIFIED**\n- **DATA PRIORITY ≠ MODEL WEIGHT**\n- **SOURCE AGREEMENT ≠ SOURCE INDEPENDENCE**\n\n### A5. Preserve Raw Evidence\n\nRaw source artifacts must remain immutable and recoverable.\n\nNormalization and correction occur downstream. The system must retain:\n\n- original source payload\n- retrieval timestamp\n- source identity\n- source-specific IDs\n- parser or collector version\n- normalized output\n- transformation lineage\n- conflict and correction history\n\nCorrections should supersede prior derived state without destroying the evidence needed to reconstruct what originally occurred.\n\n### A6. Producer–Consumer Accountability\n\nEvery required field should have:\n\n- a named producer\n- a defined schema\n- freshness expectation\n- coverage expectation\n- quality classification\n- known downstream consumers\n- fallback behavior\n- failure behavior\n\nA field appearing in a schema does not prove that a reliable producer exists. Likewise, acquiring data does not prove that it reaches the model or dashboard correctly.\n\n### A7. No Silent Fallbacks\n\nFallback behavior must be explicit and observable.\n\nThe system may not silently replace:\n\n- current data with stale cache\n- direct evidence with a proxy\n- resolved identity with name matching\n- missing information with zero\n- source conflict with arbitrary precedence\n- unavailable advanced data with an invented estimate\n\nIf a fallback is permitted, the output must identify the fallback, its reason, and its confidence impact.\n\n### A8. Coverage Is a Measured Property\n\nCoverage claims must be demonstrated at the level used by the model.\n\nMeasure separately:\n\n- event coverage\n- team coverage\n- player coverage\n- field coverage\n- historical depth\n- current-day freshness\n- identity-resolution rate\n- successful derivation rate\n- delivered-output coverage\n\nA source described as “available” may still be analytically unusable because of gaps, unstable IDs, latency, schema drift, or incomplete delivery.\n\n### A9. Schema Stability and Contract Testing\n\nFlat files, APIs, databases, and model-facing artifacts require stable contracts.\n\nRequired controls include:\n\n- required-field validation\n- data-type validation\n- enum and evidence-label validation\n- uniqueness checks\n- foreign-key or entity-reference checks\n- timestamp validation\n- snapshot identity\n- schema-version declaration\n- consumer compatibility testing\n\nSchema drift must cause a visible warning or controlled failure—not quiet corruption.\n\n### A10. Deterministic Derivation\n\nWhere feasible, the same versioned inputs and configuration must produce the same derived output.\n\nEach derived artifact should identify:\n\n- input snapshot or hashes\n- calculation version\n- configuration version\n- execution timestamp\n- evidence exclusions\n- unresolved conflicts\n- output identity\n\nThis makes disagreements diagnosable and prevents accidental movement in the baseline.\n\n### A11. Baseline Integrity\n\nBaseline represents the certified machine-readable analytical state for a particular snapshot.\n\nIt must not be silently changed by:\n\n- Don Delta\n- narrative interpretation\n- market movement\n- manual dashboard edits\n- later results\n- postmortem findings\n\nCorrections to faulty inputs may produce a new superseding baseline version, but the prior version and the reason for supersession must remain visible.\n\n### A12. Separation of Analytical Views\n\nThe system must preserve separate, addressable views for:\n\n1. source evidence\n2. normalized data\n3. certified baseline\n4. status summary\n5. market comparison\n6. Don Delta\n7. Model Synthesis\n8. final decision\n9. outcome\n10. postmortem\n\nA polished final recommendation is not a substitute for retaining the layers that produced it.\n\n### A13. Delivery Is Part of Correctness\n\nCorrect source data is not enough. The delivered artifact must be checked independently.\n\nVerification must test:\n\n- expected files and records exist\n- artifacts are readable\n- row and entity counts reconcile\n- hashes or snapshot identities match\n- consumers receive the intended versions\n- corrections propagated downstream\n- stale artifacts were not accidentally packaged\n- provenance accompanies the delivery\n\nThe final package—not merely the working directory—is the object that must pass verification.\n\n### A14. Independent Verification\n\nThe builder may test its own work, but should not be the sole certifier of material pipeline changes.\n\nThe operating sequence remains:\n\n**BUILD → SELF-TEST → PACKAGE → INDEPENDENT VERIFICATION → ACCEPT / REJECT → PROMOTE**\n\nLuce verification must use independently reproducible evidence rather than accepting completion claims at face value.\n\n### A15. Corrections and Supersession\n\nA correction must state:\n\n- what was wrong\n- affected identities, games, or records\n- root cause\n- affected downstream outputs\n- corrective action\n- regression test\n- superseded artifacts\n- current authoritative replacement\n- whether historical decisions were affected\n\nA patched value without a diagnosed failure path is not a complete correction.\n\n### A16. Daily Operational Readiness\n\nBefore any slate is analyzed, the system should produce a readiness state covering:\n\n- source availability\n- freshness\n- identity coverage\n- schedule completeness\n- personnel/availability coverage\n- expected versus actual records\n- unresolved conflicts\n- fallback activation\n- derivation success\n- packaging status\n- confidence degradations\n\nA slate may be marked:\n\n- **READY**\n- **READY WITH LIMITATIONS**\n- **HOLD**\n- **FAILED**\n\nThe model should not generate false precision from a structurally incomplete slate.\n\n### A17. Postmortems Must Separate Failure Types\n\nEvery miss or material discrepancy should be classified before methodology is changed.\n\nPotential failure classes include:\n\n- source failure\n- acquisition failure\n- identity failure\n- freshness failure\n- transformation failure\n- derivation failure\n- analytical-model failure\n- Don Delta failure\n- market-timing failure\n- execution or delivery failure\n- genuine variance despite a sound process\n\nA lost bet or incorrect projection does not automatically prove that the methodology was wrong. Likewise, a winning result does not validate a defective pipeline.\n\n### A18. Sport-Specific Adoption Boundary\n\nMLB findings may enter NCAA in two forms:\n\n1. **Cross-sport process controls** — identity, provenance, time integrity, coverage, verification, correction, delivery, and governance.\n2. **Candidate analytical concepts** — only after NCAA-specific evidence and methodology reconciliation.\n\nMLB variables, formulas, weights, thresholds, and implementation assumptions do not transfer automatically.\n\n### A19. NCAA Application\n\nFor NCAA Football, this operating layer specifically means:\n\n- transfer and roster identity require stronger controls than name matching\n- player-team affiliation must be point-in-time correct\n- coaching and coordinator identity must include role and effective dates\n- schedule and opponent-quality inputs must be versioned\n- injury and availability information must retain source and timestamp\n- weather delay and exogenous disruption must remain separate from team quality\n- market snapshots must include book, market, price, and timestamp\n- pre-Don context must expose missing, conflicted, stale, and proxy data\n- any data deficiency affecting the fair line must appear in the uncertainty band\n\n### A20. Governance Status\n\nThis addendum represents a **proposed cross-sport process promotion from MLB evidence into the NCAA operating branch**.\n\nIt does not alter NCAA methodology weights or calculations. Upon Don acceptance, it should be reconciled into Parent Quant as operating doctrine and inherited by NCAA Football without changing the sport-specific discovery gate.\n\nThat gives us the right abstraction: MLB taught us how an Edge Crew sport branch must operate from raw evidence to verified delivery. NCAA still determines what football information matters and how it should eventually be weighted.", "text_chars": 10769, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "42090bd2-0bf6-4da3-b630-159ee3c09b94", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373644.44, "updated_at": 1790373733.103368, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, buddy. I need to have some fun. It's your turn to pull up the whole slate for NCAA football this weekend, and then we'll go through the process, get you the data, and we'll talk about it here. Ground yourself in our framework first. I want to see session header, session footer,", "text_chars": 284, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cd6fb319-1bc3-49d6-b7ea-95f464af4f90", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373649.307761, "updated_at": 1790373649.307761, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fc0e436b-fae5-5b0f-8b09-98be989af25c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373656.286066, "updated_at": 1790373657.683247, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I’m grounding this as a fresh NCAA live-slate stress-test session under the frozen lifecycle. First pull is schedule identity only—matchups, kickoff times, networks/venues where verified—before we contaminate it with markets, modeling, or Don leans. I’ll keep the slate point-in-time stamped and close with a proper session footer.", "text_chars": 331, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "976d4982-5339-4723-b80d-2c9fdac8e2b1", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373657.685094, "updated_at": 1790373696.303552, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3a44cf89-41ed-450e-afc2-17b04cbbb487", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373696.289216, "updated_at": 1790373696.289216, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d3f8c00c-d7ff-5229-8f9b-23424517b8fd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373696.294187, "updated_at": 1790373733.104013, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Hell yes, Don. We’re opening this as the **second NCAA live-slate stress test**, but keeping it fun and disciplined.\n\n# SESSION HEADER\n\n**SESSION:** EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — NCAA Football Weekend Slate  \n**DATE:** Friday, September 25, 2026  \n**SLATE:** Week 4 — Friday, September 25 through Saturday, September 26  \n**PRIMARY WORKING DAY:** Saturday, September 26  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**SEAT:** Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \n**MODE:** LIVE DISCOVERY / SECOND STRESS TEST  \n**CURRENT GATE:** Schedule Intake → Slate Triage → Data Acquisition → Baseline Construction  \n**SNAPSHOT TIME:** September 25, 2026, approximately 3:00 PM Pacific  \n**SCOPE:** All games listed by CBS under its **All FBS Games** schedule  \n**SATURDAY GAME COUNT:** 65  \n**TIME DISPLAY:** Pacific time, converted from the source’s Eastern listings  \n**MARKET STATUS:** Not yet ingested  \n**DON LEAN STATUS:** Intentionally withheld  \n**BASELINE STATUS:** Not calculated  \n**MODEL SYNTHESIS:** Not opened  \n\n## Governing frame\n\nWe are following:\n\n**Schedule Identity → Data Readiness → Pre-Don Context → Baseline → Market Comparison → Don Conversation → Don Delta → Model Synthesis → Reanalysis → Result/Postmortem**\n\nThis first pull establishes only:\n\n- matchup identity\n- home/away orientation\n- kickoff time\n- broadcast assignment\n- source timestamp\n\nIt does **not** yet establish:\n\n- spreads or totals\n- fair lines\n- team strength\n- injury status\n- player availability\n- Don candidates\n- recommended plays\n\nNo early Don lean gets solicited before the context board exists. We specifically learned from LSU–Ole Miss and USC–Rutgers that an ungrounded lean can anchor the entire analysis.\n\n# Weekend opening games\n\nThursday’s Liberty–Coastal Carolina game is already complete. Friday includes Army–Temple, Navy–UAB, Howard–Rutgers, Northwestern–Indiana, and Clemson–California. genui{\"citation\":{\"ref\":\"turn2view0\"}}\n\n| Pacific | Matchup | Network | State at snapshot |\n|---:|---|---|---|\n| Earlier Friday | Army at Temple | ESPN | Already underway at source capture |\n| 4:00 PM | Navy at UAB | ESPN | Pregame |\n| 4:00 PM | Howard at Rutgers | BTN | Pregame |\n| 5:00 PM | Northwestern at No. 5 Indiana | FOX | Pregame |\n| 7:30 PM | Clemson at California | ESPN | Pregame |\n\nThose can remain observational unless one jumps out as a useful mini-test. The full controlled slate is Saturday.\n\n# Saturday, September 26 — Complete FBS Slate\n\n## 9:00–10:30 AM Pacific window\n\nThe source lists twelve games through the 9:45 AM start and four additional games from 10:00–11:00 AM. genui{\"citation\":{\"ref\":\"turn2view0\"}}\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 9:00 AM | Sam Houston at No. 11 Texas Tech | TNT |\n| 9:00 AM | Bucknell at Pittsburgh | ACCN |\n| 9:00 AM | Colorado at Baylor | ESPN2 |\n| 9:00 AM | Colorado State at UTSA | ESPNU |\n| 9:00 AM | Illinois at No. 7 Ohio State | FOX |\n| 9:00 AM | Ball State at Kent State | ESPN+ |\n| 9:00 AM | San Diego State at Toledo | CBSSN |\n| 9:00 AM | No. 1 Texas at No. 14 Tennessee | ABC |\n| 9:00 AM | UNLV at Akron | ESPN+ |\n| 9:00 AM | Virginia Tech at Boston College | ACCN |\n| 9:00 AM | Wake Forest at No. 16 Louisville | ESPN |\n| 9:45 AM | South Alabama at Kentucky | SECN |\n| 10:00 AM | Lindenwood at Eastern Michigan | ESPN+ |\n| 10:30 AM | UCLA at Maryland | BTN |\n\n## 11:00 AM–Noon Pacific window\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 11:00 AM | Northern Illinois at Georgia State | ESPN+ |\n| 11:00 AM | No. 3 Notre Dame at Purdue | Peacock |\n| Noon | Central Arkansas at Florida State | ACCN |\n| Noon | Hawaii at Wyoming | CW |\n\n## 12:30 PM Pacific window\n\nCBS lists twelve games in the 3:30 PM Eastern block, including three ranked-versus-ranked matchups. genui{\"citation\":{\"ref\":\"turn2view1\"}}\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 12:30 PM | Gardner-Webb at Marshall | ESPN+ |\n| 12:30 PM | William & Mary at Duke | ACCN |\n| 12:30 PM | No. 15 Utah at Iowa State | FOX |\n| 12:30 PM | UConn at Miami (Ohio) | ESPN+ |\n| 12:30 PM | TCU at UCF | FS1 |\n| 12:30 PM | Stonehill at Ohio | ESPN+ |\n| 12:30 PM | Robert Morris at Buffalo | ESPN+ |\n| 12:30 PM | Oklahoma at No. 2 Georgia | ESPN |\n| 12:30 PM | New Mexico at New Mexico State | CBSSN |\n| 12:30 PM | No. 4 Ole Miss at No. 21 Florida | ABC |\n| 12:30 PM | No. 17 Iowa at No. 18 Michigan | Network not shown |\n| 12:30 PM | Boise State at Western Michigan | ESPN2 |\n\n## 1:00–3:30 PM Pacific window\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 1:00 PM | NC Central at East Carolina | ESPN+ |\n| 1:00 PM | No. 25 Houston at Georgia Southern | ESPNU |\n| 1:15 PM | Vanderbilt at Auburn | SECN |\n| 2:00 PM | Wisconsin at No. 13 Penn State | Peacock |\n| 2:00 PM | South Florida at Bowling Green | ESPN+ |\n| 2:00 PM | Nebraska at Michigan State | BTN |\n| 3:00 PM | Incarnate Word at Texas State | USA |\n| 3:00 PM | LIU at FIU | ESPN+ |\n| 3:00 PM | James Madison at Old Dominion | ESPN+ |\n| 3:00 PM | Delaware at Virginia | ACCN |\n| 3:30 PM | Louisiana at Charlotte | ESPN+ |\n| 3:30 PM | Central Michigan at No. 6 Miami | CW |\n\n## 4:00 PM Pacific window\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 4:00 PM | Mercyhurst at Western Kentucky | ESPN+ |\n| 4:00 PM | Kennesaw State at Arkansas State | ESPN+ |\n| 4:00 PM | Kansas State at Cincinnati | ESPN2 |\n| 4:00 PM | Middle Tennessee at Jacksonville State | ESPN+ |\n| 4:00 PM | Oklahoma State at West Virginia | FS1 |\n| 4:00 PM | South Carolina at No. 8 Alabama | ESPN |\n| 4:00 PM | Southern Miss at Tulane | ESPN+ |\n\n## 4:30–5:00 PM Pacific window\n\nThe national evening block contains Texas A&M–LSU, Oregon–USC, Missouri–Mississippi State, and several potentially useful mid-tier matchup tests. genui{\"citation\":{\"ref\":\"turn2view2\"}}\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 4:30 PM | No. 23 Texas A&M at No. 10 LSU | ABC |\n| 4:30 PM | Troy at Utah State | CBSSN |\n| 4:30 PM | No. 20 Oregon at No. 12 USC | NBC |\n| 4:30 PM | Houston Christian at North Texas | ESPN+ |\n| 4:30 PM | Arizona at Washington State | Network not shown |\n| 4:30 PM | Appalachian State at NC State | ESPNU |\n| 4:45 PM | No. 19 Missouri at No. 24 Mississippi State | SECN |\n| 5:00 PM | Tulsa at Arkansas | SEC Network+ |\n| 5:00 PM | Florida Atlantic at UL Monroe | ESPN+ |\n\n## 6:00–8:00 PM Pacific window\n\n| PT | Matchup | Network |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 6:00 PM | UMass at Sacramento State | ESPN+ |\n| 6:00 PM | Oregon State at UTEP | Mountain West Network |\n| 6:00 PM | Missouri State at No. 22 SMU | ACCN |\n| 7:00 PM | Rice at Fresno State | CW |\n| 7:30 PM | Air Force at Nevada | FS1 |\n| 7:30 PM | Georgia Tech at Stanford | ESPN |\n| 8:00 PM | Minnesota at Washington | FOX |\n\nCBS explicitly labels its displayed schedule times as Eastern; I converted them to Pacific for our working board. genui{\"citation\":{\"ref\":\"turn2view3\"}}\n\n# First-pass slate structure\n\nWithout introducing spreads or making selections, the slate naturally divides into these analytical lanes:\n\n| Lane | Examples | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| Elite head-to-head | Texas–Tennessee, Oklahoma–Georgia, Oregon–USC | Strong opponent-adjustment test |\n| Ranked but potentially mispriced | Utah–Iowa State, Iowa–Michigan, Missouri–Mississippi State | Reputation versus current evidence |\n| Large favorite/margin sustainability | Illinois–Ohio State, Wake–Louisville, South Carolina–Alabama | Direct continuation of the first stress-test lesson |\n| Competitive mid-tier | Colorado–Baylor, TCU–UCF, Kansas State–Cincinnati | Less brand distortion, useful model test |\n| G5 discovery | SDSU–Toledo, Boise–Western Michigan, USF–Bowling Green | Data sufficiency and identity-depth test |\n| Situational/environment | Hawaii–Wyoming, Arizona–Washington State, Minnesota–Washington | Travel, altitude, venue, time-zone and style effects |\n| FCS/FBS control group | Bucknell–Pitt, Central Arkansas–FSU, William & Mary–Duke | Usually lower analytical priority unless market structure is unusual |\n\n## Proposed working funnel\n\nWe should not try to fully research all 65 games equally. The proper edge-to-edge process is:\n\n1. **Validate all 65 game identities.**\n2. **Acquire one point-in-time market snapshot for all 65.**\n3. **Run broad schedule, team-form, personnel and source-readiness screening.**\n4. **Identify approximately 12–18 games with a plausible modeled question.**\n5. **Produce full two-team context boards for perhaps 8–12.**\n6. **Run Baseline without Don input.**\n7. **Surface the strongest case for both sides.**\n8. **Ask for Don’s lean only after the player and context layer is visible.**\n9. **Complete the two-sided Don Delta conversation.**\n10. **Allow pass/no-play to eliminate weak edges.**\n\nMy first instinct for the **research board**, not a betting board, is to retain:\n\n- Texas at Tennessee\n- Illinois at Ohio State\n- Wake Forest at Louisville\n- Colorado at Baylor\n- Utah at Iowa State\n- TCU at UCF\n- Oklahoma at Georgia\n- Ole Miss at Florida\n- Iowa at Michigan\n- Boise State at Western Michigan\n- Vanderbilt at Auburn\n- Kansas State at Cincinnati\n- South Carolina at Alabama\n- Texas A&M at LSU\n- Oregon at USC\n- Missouri at Mississippi State\n- Georgia Tech at Stanford\n- Minnesota at Washington\n\nThat is an **18-game preliminary research universe**. It remains pre-market and contains no side recommendations.\n\n# SESSION FOOTER\n\n**SESSION STATE:** OPEN  \n**SLATE IDENTITY:** INGESTED — 65 Saturday games  \n**SOURCE STATE:** One complete FBS schedule source located; ESPN schedule presence also confirmed  \n**TIME NORMALIZATION:** Eastern source times converted to Pacific  \n**MARKET DATA:** NOT INGESTED  \n**TEAM DATA:** NOT INGESTED  \n**PLAYER/PERSONNEL DATA:** NOT INGESTED  \n**INJURY DATA:** NOT INGESTED  \n**WEATHER:** NOT INGESTED  \n**BASELINE:** NOT RUN  \n**DON LEAN:** NOT REQUESTED  \n**DON DELTA:** NOT OPEN  \n**MODEL SYNTHESIS:** NOT OPEN  \n**DECISIONS/PLAYS:** NONE  \n**CONTAMINATION CHECK:** PASS — schedule information only  \n**NEXT ACTION:** Capture the initial market board across all 65 games, then perform slate triage before deep research.  \n**GOVERNANCE NOTE:** The 18-game list is a discovery funnel, not canon, certification, or a recommended betting card.", "text_chars": 10095, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "85a258f0-17fe-45e5-a6e7-926e8bdc294a", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374817.903738, "updated_at": 1790374882.900908, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, this is actually cool because there's so many games. What we should do is define a first pass to calculate very high edge or arbitrage or expected value. To first do a pass. All the top 25 in major conferences games, we should review, but we should do a first pass to rank them on what we should see and what we should dig into. Then we'll run the data on everything, but we could trim down the game slate with some kind of first pass.", "text_chars": 441, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9ba3dd21-5621-4496-a251-babcfbd5f4b1", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374818.745715, "updated_at": 1790374818.745715, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c86c9af3-fe5b-5fee-9c31-ca85284f3ca3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374831.114922, "updated_at": 1790374833.060817, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3d2b51ad-b710-58fe-8bf0-1f1aab59bba8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374841.050553, "updated_at": 1790374841.585221, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b56b283b-1556-4a5a-8213-2736cda9e6b6", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374841.588901, "updated_at": 1790374841.588901, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "49f340b3-81e1-5601-b5b9-358db8ca752a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374841.593682, "updated_at": 1790374882.901189, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "# SESSION HEADER\n\n**SESSION:** EC⁸ NCAA Football — Slate Triage Design  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**MODE:** METHODOLOGY DISCOVERY / LIVE STRESS TEST  \n**CURRENT GATE:** Full Slate → Quantitative First Pass → Ranked Research Queue  \n**BASELINE:** Not run  \n**MARKETS:** Not yet captured  \n**DON LEAN:** Withheld  \n**DECISIONS:** None  \n\nYes—that is exactly how we make 65 games manageable without prematurely throwing games away.\n\nThe first pass should not pretend to be the final model. Its job is to identify:\n\n1. Possible high expected value\n2. Genuine arbitrage or executable middles\n3. Market/model disagreement\n4. Games with high analytical information value\n5. Games requiring mandatory review regardless of the initial score\n\n# Proposed First-Pass Architecture\n\n## Lane 1 — Mandatory Review\n\nThese games survive automatically:\n\n- Every game involving an AP Top-25 team\n- Every ranked-versus-ranked game\n- Every Power Four conference game\n- Major rivalry games\n- Games with major QB, coaching, injury, weather, or venue changes\n- Games with extreme market movement\n- Any game Don explicitly wants retained\n\nMandatory review does not mean mandatory deep modeling or betting. It means the game cannot disappear because a rough screen found nothing.\n\n## Lane 2 — Whole-Slate Opportunity Scan\n\nAll 65 games receive the same inexpensive initial calculation.\n\n### Minimum first-pass inputs\n\nFor each game:\n\n- Consensus spread, total and moneyline\n- Best available spread, total and moneyline\n- Opener and current price\n- Book and timestamp\n- Current team efficiency\n- Opponent-adjusted performance where available\n- Offensive and defensive scoring efficiency\n- Explosive-play profile\n- Success-rate or available proxy\n- Turnover performance and turnover luck\n- QB identity and broad QB stability\n- Recent schedule quality\n- Home/away and venue\n- Rest and travel\n- Known coaching or personnel disruption\n- Data completeness and freshness\n\nThis is not yet the full player/context package. It is the broad substrate required to detect where deeper work may pay.\n\n# Three Separate Opportunity Tests\n\n## 1. True Arbitrage\n\nArbitrage must remain separate from model edge.\n\nFor a two-outcome market:\n\n\\[\nA=\\frac{1}{O_A}+\\frac{1}{O_B}\n\\]\n\nWhere \\(O_A\\) and \\(O_B\\) are the best available decimal prices at different books.\n\n- \\(A < 1.00\\): theoretical arbitrage\n- \\(A < 0.99\\): potentially executable after a safety buffer\n- \\(A \\geq 1.00\\): not an arbitrage\n\nThe board must retain:\n\n- book on each side\n- exact price\n- timestamp\n- available limit, if known\n- expected return\n- execution risk\n- whether both prices were simultaneously available\n\nA stale price paired with a current price is not arbitrage.\n\n### Separate labels\n\n- **TRUE ARBITRAGE**\n- **POTENTIAL ARBITRAGE — NEEDS LIVE CONFIRMATION**\n- **MIDDLE OPPORTUNITY**\n- **SCALP / PRICE IMPROVEMENT**\n- **NOT ARBITRAGE**\n\nA spread middle is not guaranteed arbitrage unless the available lines and prices create a mathematically lockable outcome.\n\n## 2. Provisional Expected Value\n\nThe first-pass model generates a deliberately rough:\n\n- fair spread\n- fair total\n- fair moneyline probability\n- uncertainty band\n\nFor a moneyline or binary proposition:\n\n\\[\nEV = p_{\\text{model}}(O-1)-(1-p_{\\text{model}})\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n\n- \\(p_{\\text{model}}\\) is the provisional win probability\n- \\(O\\) is the available decimal price\n\nFor spreads and totals, we estimate cover probability from the provisional margin or total distribution—not merely from the difference between two lines.\n\n### First-pass EV bands\n\n| Band | Provisional EV | Interpretation |\n|---|---:|---|\n| A | 5%+ | High-priority investigation |\n| B | 2.5%–4.9% | Plausible edge |\n| C | 0%–2.4% | Thin or price-sensitive |\n| D | Below 0% | No initial value |\n\nThese bands rank research priority. They are not yet betting thresholds.\n\n## 3. Model–Market Dislocation\n\nFor spreads:\n\n\\[\nG_S = \\text{Provisional Fair Spread} - \\text{Market Spread}\n\\]\n\nFor totals:\n\n\\[\nG_T = \\text{Provisional Fair Total} - \\text{Market Total}\n\\]\n\nInitial investigation bands:\n\n| Absolute gap | First-pass state |\n|---:|---|\n| 4.0+ points | Major dislocation |\n| 2.5–3.9 | Material dislocation |\n| 1.0–2.4 | Mild disagreement |\n| Under 1.0 | Market alignment |\n\nBecause uncertainty varies by game, the better companion calculation is:\n\n\\[\nZ_{\\text{edge}}=\n\\frac{|\\text{Fair}-\\text{Market}|}\n{\\text{First-pass uncertainty}}\n\\]\n\nA four-point gap with enormous uncertainty may deserve less attention than a three-point gap supported by stable data.\n\n# Confidence-Adjusted Edge\n\nThe screen should not rank raw gaps alone.\n\n\\[\nCAE = EV_{\\text{raw}}\n\\times R_{\\text{data}}\n\\times R_{\\text{identity}}\n\\times R_{\\text{freshness}}\n\\times R_{\\text{model}}\n\\]\n\nEach reliability term ranges from 0 to 1.\n\nExample:\n\n- Raw EV: 7%\n- Data reliability: 0.90\n- Identity reliability: 1.00\n- Freshness: 0.95\n- Provisional-model reliability: 0.65\n\n\\[\nCAE \\approx 3.9\\%\n\\]\n\nThat correctly prevents a loud but fragile early model from outranking a smaller, better-supported edge.\n\n# Research Priority Score\n\nWe need a score that answers **“Where should we dig?”**, not “What should we bet?”\n\nProposed discovery-stage score:\n\n| Component | Points |\n|---|---:|\n| Confidence-adjusted EV | 0–30 |\n| Fair-line/market dislocation | 0–20 |\n| Cross-book price dispersion | 0–10 |\n| Opponent-adjusted performance conflict | 0–10 |\n| Matchup-interaction extremity | 0–10 |\n| Personnel or coaching leverage | 0–5 |\n| Margin-sustainability signal | 0–5 |\n| Situational/environmental leverage | 0–5 |\n| Data readiness | 0–5 |\n| **Maximum** | **100** |\n\nThese are **triage weights only**. They do not become NCAA methodology weights.\n\n## Priority result\n\n| Score | Disposition |\n|---:|---|\n| 75–100 | **RED — Immediate deep dive** |\n| 60–74 | **ORANGE — Strong research candidate** |\n| 45–59 | **YELLOW — Secondary review** |\n| 30–44 | **BLUE — Monitor/price dependent** |\n| Under 30 | **GRAY — Initial pass** |\n\n# Mandatory Overrides\n\nA game is promoted regardless of score when it has:\n\n- true-arbitrage evidence\n- a ranked-versus-ranked matchup\n- an extreme line move\n- a starting-QB change\n- material late injury news\n- major weather disruption\n- a source conflict affecting key personnel\n- severe market dispersion\n- unusual favorite-margin structure\n- explicit Don interest\n\nThat protects us from allowing a cheap model to suppress the exact contextual signals NCAA football is sensitive to.\n\n# First-Pass Output Board\n\nEvery game gets one row:\n\n| Field | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Game ID | Stable identity |\n| Matchup / kickoff | Schedule state |\n| Mandatory-review reason | Top 25, P4, rivalry, Don, disruption |\n| Consensus spread/total/ML | Market reference |\n| Best available prices | Executable market |\n| Opener/current movement | Market evolution |\n| Provisional fair spread/total | Broad baseline |\n| Raw gap | Model-market disagreement |\n| Provisional EV | Initial value |\n| Confidence-adjusted EV | Reliability-aware value |\n| Arbitrage status | Separate executable-price test |\n| Data readiness | Coverage and freshness |\n| Primary signal | Why it ranked |\n| Counter-signal | Why it may be false |\n| Uncertainty | Visible weakness |\n| Priority score/tier | Research order |\n| Disposition | Deep dive, review, monitor or pass |\n\n# Funnel for This Slate\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"65-game FBS slate\"] --> B[\"Market + broad-data scan\"]\n    B --> C[\"Mandatory-review pool\"]\n    B --> D[\"Opportunity-ranked pool\"]\n    C --> E[\"Combined research board\"]\n    D --> E\n    E --> F[\"Approximately 15–25 retained\"]\n    F --> G[\"Full data packages\"]\n    G --> H[\"Approximately 8–12 serious candidates\"]\n    H --> I[\"Baseline + two-sided context\"]\n    I --> J[\"Don Delta\"]\n```\n\nThe 15–25 retained games are not picks. They are the games where the cost of deeper analysis appears justified.\n\n# Recommended First-Pass Order\n\n1. Capture simultaneous market snapshots for all 65.\n2. Flag every Top-25 and Power Four conference game.\n3. Run the arbitrage and cross-book dispersion scan across everything.\n4. Generate the broad provisional fair lines and totals.\n5. Apply confidence and data-readiness penalties.\n6. Produce the ranked research board.\n7. Review the cutoff together.\n8. Acquire deeper team/player data for the retained pool.\n9. Only then open individual Baselines and Don conversations.\n\nThis gives us both things we want:\n\n- **Complete-slate coverage**\n- **Aggressive reduction of wasted deep-research effort**\n\n# SESSION FOOTER\n\n**SESSION STATE:** OPEN  \n**SLATE:** 65 Saturday games  \n**FIRST-PASS DESIGN:** DEFINED — pending Don acceptance  \n**MANDATORY COVERAGE:** Top 25 + Power Four conference games + overrides  \n**ARBITRAGE:** Separate mathematical lane  \n**EXPECTED VALUE:** Provisional and confidence-adjusted  \n**TRIAGE SCORE:** Research-priority tool only  \n**NCAA WEIGHTS:** Unchanged / not locked  \n**MARKETS:** Not yet captured  \n**BASELINES:** Not run  \n**DON LEANS:** Not requested  \n**NEXT ACTION:** Capture a synchronized market snapshot for the complete slate and generate the first ranked board.", "text_chars": 9064, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "34058925-e486-46f7-a322-76d4c011eec0", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374944.3587341, "updated_at": 1790375538.372575, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, that's dope. That's dope. Update your NCAA artifact, right, within Google Drive, and go ahead and first scan Google Drive because we did a kind of a reorg and do a recon there for anything NCAA football and give me the current state of the artifacts in Google Drive.", "text_chars": 272, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "77ec427f-d510-4989-a4e2-5810bcfceb20", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790374945.040517, "updated_at": 1790374945.040517, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5.6-sol-wm", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractab